Subject: [PATCH] core: make the analyze() switch volatile

sc_core_skip_analyze is a global written by the `anlz` debug verb and read by
`state`, which the measurement harness uses to verify the flag actually
changed before sampling. Without volatile the compiler is free to cache the
read, so the harness can see a stale value and discard the pair -- or worse,
proceed on a value that does not reflect the toggle.

The first harness run against the analyze() switch discarded all 16 pairs on
"flag did not switch", which is this bug surfacing rather than a real failure.
A harness that cannot confirm its own precondition is worse than no harness:
it produces an exit code and no number.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
--- a/src/simcity_static_recomp.c
+++ b/src/simcity_static_recomp.c
@@ -101,7 +101,7 @@
     return g_core_log != NULL;
 }
 
-int sc_core_skip_analyze = 0;
+volatile int sc_core_skip_analyze = 0;
 void sc_core_set_skip_analyze(int skip) { sc_core_skip_analyze = skip ? 1 : 0; }
 int sc_core_get_skip_analyze(void) { return sc_core_skip_analyze; }
 
